1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,190
Have you ever heard the phrase Eat your own dog food?

2
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:07,105
This is also known as dogfooding

3
00:00:07,105 --> 00:00:11,085
or the idea that you and your company should use your own products.

4
00:00:11,085 --> 00:00:15,210
This is part of the culture at many large companies like Google and Uber.

5
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:17,340
And also at smaller startups as well.

6
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:21,870
Employees will get access to pre-release versions of products to use,

7
00:00:21,870 --> 00:00:24,285
test, and provide feedback.

8
00:00:24,285 --> 00:00:27,120
How is this different from user testing?

9
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,075
Well, user testing can be helpful,

10
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:33,270
but oftentimes, lab environments are too controlled.

11
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:39,085
Or user testing is done using a prototype that doesn't have complete functionality.

12
00:00:39,085 --> 00:00:43,375
The product is also used for just a short period of time.

13
00:00:43,375 --> 00:00:46,820
This prevents you from getting important feedback.

14
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,780
Dogfooding helps to solve that problem because it allows

15
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:54,655
you to get feedback on your product based on real-world usage.

16
00:00:54,655 --> 00:00:59,395
Dog fooders use a real version of their product, not a prototype.

17
00:00:59,395 --> 00:01:02,930
When they run into issues or bugs, they report them.

18
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:06,140
This directly helps to improve your product.

19
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:10,615
Dogfooders also use the product for an extended period of time,

20
00:01:10,615 --> 00:01:13,040
so have more of an opportunity to use

21
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:17,315
more features of the product and test them out in different scenarios.

22
00:01:17,315 --> 00:01:22,085
Dogfooding is a way to identify issues before launching your product.

23
00:01:22,085 --> 00:01:25,100
These issues could be bugs where there's a problem with

24
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:28,610
the code not working correctly or doing what it's supposed to.

25
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:32,200
These issues will sometimes be identified through QA.

26
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,300
However, dogfooding will also help you

27
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:39,160
identify issues with the product when it comes to user expectations.

28
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:41,630
For example, if the product doesn't work the way

29
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:44,765
a user would expect or doesn't meet a user's needs.

30
00:01:44,765 --> 00:01:50,730
This type of feedback is critical and not something that would be discovered through QA.

